# PaceTasks

Expo (React Native) + TypeScript task app backed by Supabase. Anonymous
auth first — no login wall, each device gets a stable Supabase user id via
`ensureSession()` in `src/lib/supabase.ts`; an optional account (Settings)
attaches an email to that same user. The UI is calm and card-based: one
system sans everywhere (hierarchy from weight and size), three color themes
(Sage, Pastel Night, Sunny) each in light and dark (see `src/lib/theme.ts` and
`src/hooks/useTheme.tsx`), and a text-only pill tab bar (see `App.tsx`'s
`PillTabBar`). Design work happens on the long-lived `ui-design-w/Meltem`
branch and reaches `main` by pull request; that branch is never deleted.

## Structure

- `App.tsx` — root: wraps the app in `ThemeProvider`, waits for session, sets up the 3-tab navigator (Tasks / Stats / Settings) behind a custom `PillTabBar` (text-only, no icons).
- `src/screens/` — one file per tab screen. Screens own layout + hook wiring, no direct Supabase calls.
- `src/components/` — presentational pieces reused across screens (AddTaskSheet, TaskItem, FocusSessionModal, EndOfDayCard, WeekHeatmap, DropdownPill, ChoiceChips, Stepper, StatCard, BrainDumpSheet, DoNowSheet, OneThingSheet, RolloverCard, TodayHero, WeekStrip, TodayFilterBar, PickNextTaskSheet, SomedaySheet, WeeklyResetSheet, AccountCard, AccountSheet, DataCard, DayPicker, DeleteAccountSheet, TextField, ErrorBanner, Button). No data fetching here.
- `src/hooks/` — `useSession`, `useTasks`, `useSettings`. The user can change while the app is open (sign-in, sign-out, account deletion): `useTasks` and `useSettings` drop the previous user's data the moment `userId` changes, and `useSession` starts a missing session again when the app returns to the foreground (`reviveSession`). A screen that returns early while loading must still render its sheets (see `sheets` in `SettingsScreen`), or a sheet showing a result is torn down by the reload. All Supabase reads/writes for a screen go through these, never inline in a component. `useTasks` also keeps its list live (Realtime INSERT/UPDATE filtered by user, unfiltered DELETE, silent reload on app foreground) and merges every change through `upsertTask`/`removeTask` so a local write and its Realtime echo never duplicate. Both `TaskListScreen` and `SettingsScreen` call `useSettings` independently (each screen owns its own data, per convention) rather than prop-drilling settings down from `App.tsx`.
- Brain Dump UI: `useBrainDump` (hook) drives `BrainDumpSheet` + `BrainDumpCandidateRow` (presentational). All transitions live in the pure reducer `src/lib/brainDumpState.ts` (unit-tested) — add new states/actions there, not in the hook. `src/lib/brainDumpApi.ts` is the only caller of the `brain-dump` Edge Function and the commit/discard RPCs; it always sends the phone's time zone (`src/lib/deviceContext.ts`). UI wording for issue codes and summaries lives in `src/lib/brainDumpCopy.ts`; due-day labels in `src/lib/dueLabel.ts`.
- Today screen layout: `TodayHero` (encouraging line from `src/lib/heroCopy.ts`, totals from `src/lib/todayCopy.ts`, the one "Pick my next task" button), `WeekStrip` (read-only week from the pure `src/lib/weekStrip.ts`), the collapsed `RolloverCard` row, then `TodayFilterBar` (All / Quick wins / Due today via `filterTodayTasks` in `src/lib/taskSections.ts`; its filter button holds the groupings). `PickNextTaskSheet` is the chooser behind the hero button: "Decide for me" opens One Thing mode, "Fit my time" opens "What can I do now?". Every number on Today comes from `summarizePending`.
- "What can I do now?" (shown to users as "Fit my time"): `useDoNow` (hook: the picked time and energy plus the live suggestions) drives `DoNowSheet` (presentational), which renders rows through the screen's own `renderTaskItem`, so timers and completion behave exactly as on Today. All rules live in the pure `@domain/doNow` (`fitsNow`, `selectDoNowTasks`: filter by time and energy, rank, fill the time) — change ranking there, never in the UI, because One Thing mode and Siri will reuse it. Wording lives in `src/lib/doNowCopy.ts`; energy labels in `src/lib/energy.ts`.
- One Thing mode (shown to users as "Decide for me"): `useOneThing` (hook: the current task, the skipped set, the choosing → confirming → completed phases) drives `OneThingSheet` (presentational). The queue comes from `selectOneThingQueue` in `@domain/doNow`, which shares `rankTasks` with "What can I do now?" so both always agree. Wording lives in `src/lib/oneThingCopy.ts`.
- Rollover ("I didn't do it"): `useRollover` (hook: proposals, the user's destination overrides, apply, dismiss-until-tomorrow via `src/lib/rolloverStorage.ts`) drives `RolloverCard` (presentational: one slim "N tasks carried over · Review" row in the Today list's header that expands into the proposals). Rules live in the pure `@domain/rollover` (`isUnfinished`, `proposeRollover`, `rolloverPatch`, thresholds in `ROLLOVER_POLICY`); wording in `src/lib/rolloverCopy.ts`.
- Weekly Reset: `useWeeklyReset` (hook) drives `WeeklyResetSheet` (presentational). Which tasks qualify and what each decision does live in the pure `@domain/weeklyReset` (`selectResetItems`, `resetPatch`, `resetOutcome`, thresholds in `WEEKLY_RESET_POLICY`); the intro → reviewing → summary transitions in the pure `src/lib/weeklyResetState.ts` (unit-tested — the items are a snapshot taken at the start, so decisions never reshuffle the run); wording in `src/lib/weeklyResetCopy.ts`. Move decisions reuse the rollover destinations.
- Moving and parking tasks: never build an update object inline. Use the patches in `@domain/taskPatch` (`PARK_PATCH`, `UNPARK_PATCH`, `reschedulePatch`) with `useTasks().update` / `updateMany` → `tasksApi.updateTask`. Parking clears the due date, so a task brought back is never instantly overdue.
- Someday: `selectSomedayTasks` (`@domain/todayTasks`) feeds `SomedaySheet`; tasks are parked from `TaskItem`'s menu (`onPark`).
- Accounts are passwordless and optional. `useAccount(session)` drives `AccountCard` (in Settings) and `AccountSheet`; transitions live in the pure reducer `src/lib/accountState.ts` (unit-tested), validation in `@domain/account`, wording in `src/lib/accountCopy.ts`. `src/lib/accountApi.ts` is the only caller of `supabase.auth.updateUser / signInWithOtp / verifyOtp / signOut`, of the `profiles` table and of the `delete_account` function. Sign-up = `updateUser({ email, data })` on the anonymous user + `verifyOtp(type "email_change")`, so the user id (and every task) is kept; never create a second user for sign-up. Sign-up details ride in `user_metadata` so `useAccount` can create the missing `profiles` row on a later start.
- Deleting the account: `DataCard` (Settings, "Your data") opens `DeleteAccountSheet`; `useAccount` exposes `deletion` / `openDeletion` / `confirmDeletion` / `closeDeletion`, with transitions in the pure reducer `src/lib/accountDeletionState.ts` (unit-tested) and wording in `accountCopy.ts`. The sheet remembers the user it asked about and closes if the session changes, so it can never delete a different user. `accountApi.deleteAccount()` calls the `delete_account` database function (deletes the caller's `auth.users` row; every user table cascades from it), then starts a fresh anonymous session. It works with or without a signed-up account. A new table holding user data must reference `auth.users (id) on delete cascade`, and gets a line in `supabase/tests/database/delete_account.test.sql`.
- Public pages live in `docs/` (GitHub Pages): `privacy.html`, `support.html`, `index.html`, sharing `site.css`. Their addresses are defined once in `src/lib/links.ts`; open them with `openExternalLink`. Any change to what is stored about a user (a new table or column, an AI provider, analytics) must update `docs/privacy.html` in the same change.
- `profiles` holds first name, last name and marketing consent only; the email lives in `auth.users`. `marketing_opt_in` must never default to or be pre-set to true, and `marketing_opt_in_at` is owned by the `profiles_stamp` trigger.
- `src/components/TextField.tsx` — the labelled form input with its error line; use it for any new form field.
- `src/components/Button.tsx` — the full-width pill button for every sheet (`variant="primary"` for the one main action, `"secondary"` for the calmer alternatives, `"destructive"` for deleting). Don't hand-roll another button style in a sheet.
- `useTasks` mutations (`create`, `complete`, `update`, `updateMany`, `remove`, `clearCompleted`) never throw: they go through its `run` helper, which reports a failure through `error` and resolves `false` (`create` resolves the created task, or `undefined` on failure, so the add sheet can offer Undo). Add new mutations the same way.
- `src/lib/tasksApi.ts` / `src/lib/settingsApi.ts` — the only files that call Supabase directly (besides `brainDumpApi.ts` for Brain Dump). Tasks are created only via `createTask(draft)` → `rpc("create_task")`; never insert into `tasks` directly, since Brain Dump and Siri share that one creation path.
- `supabase/functions/_shared/domain/` — pure, dependency-free TypeScript shared by the app (imported as `@domain/...`) and Edge Functions (Deno). `task.ts` (task model, enums, limits, `TaskDraft`), `dates.ts` (local date keys — "today" always comes from the phone), `todayTasks.ts` (what the Today list/Siri show), `doNow.ts` ("What can I do now?" rules), `timing.ts` (`timingForHour`: an hour → before/after-work bucket), `rollover.ts` (where unfinished tasks go), `weeklyReset.ts` (which tasks need a weekly decision), `taskPatch.ts` (the only ways a task is moved or parked), `taskCollection.ts`, `taskHistory.ts`. Relative imports inside it must use explicit `.ts` extensions and `import type` for types, or Deno breaks. Test fixtures live in `domain/testing/`.
- `src/lib/paceInsights.ts` — pure functions only: `computePaceInsights` ("My Pace": best hours, strongest weekday, estimate bias; thresholds in `PACE_INSIGHTS_POLICY`). Sentences come from `src/lib/paceInsightsCopy.ts`; `PaceInsightsCard` on the Stats tab only renders them. Add new insights here, each with a minimum amount of history before it speaks.
- `src/lib/stats.ts` — pure functions only (no React, no network): `computeStats` (streaks/accuracy) and `buildWeekdayHeatmap` (the Stats tab's Mon-Fri × N-week grid).
- `@domain/taskHistory` — pure functions only, groups tasks by normalized (trimmed, lowercased) title. Powers the add sheet's autocomplete and auto-fill (time, timing, category, energy), and `TaskItem`'s range-timer bounds (`findExactMatch` looked up per task in `TaskListScreen`).
- `src/lib/categories.ts` — fixed list of task categories (`id`, `label`, `icon`, `color`). A task's `category` field stores a category `id` or `null` ("No category"). Add new categories here, never inline in a component.
- `src/components/DropdownPill.tsx` — generic one-tap choice control (Modal-based) used for the per-task destination in `RolloverCard`. Reuse it for any compact, secondary "pick one of a few options" control inside a row.
- `src/components/ChoiceChips.tsx` — a row of always-visible, single-tap chips. Use it only where the choice is the screen's main question (time and energy in `DoNowSheet`); everywhere else prefer `DropdownPill`.
- `src/components/TaskItem.tsx` — owns two independent timers: a manual actual-minutes stepper (always available via the checkbox) and, for tasks without a `scheduled_time`, a lightweight range timer (tap the minutes pill) that counts down from `suggestedMax` and flags "in range" once past `suggestedMin` — both props computed by the caller (`TaskListScreen`) from `taskHistory`, not fetched inside the component.
- `src/components/FocusSessionModal.tsx` — the Pomodoro countdown, opened from a task's FOCUS badge (shown when `task.scheduled_time` is set — mutually exclusive with the range timer). Reaching zero calls `onComplete` with `actual_minutes = workMinutes` — don't change that without considering the "estimate accuracy" stat, which depends on real actual-vs-estimated data.
- `src/lib/theme.ts` — single source of colors/spacing/radius/typography: `buildTheme(scheme, accentId, paletteId)` returns the active `AppTheme`: the chosen color theme's light/dark base colors, its accent (only the Sage theme takes the user's accent pick; see `paletteUsesAccent`) and `theme.hero`, the colors for the Today hero card and week strip. `theme.fonts.display` and `theme.fonts.mono` both point at the system sans; number styles add `fontVariant: ["tabular-nums"]` so digits line up. Corners use `theme.radius` by role (sm tags, md rows and tiles, lg cards and sheets, pill buttons). There is no static `theme` export. Never hardcode a hex color in a component; add it to the palettes instead (text on an accent fill is `theme.colors.onAccent`, a scrim is `theme.colors.overlay`).
- `src/hooks/useTheme.tsx` — `ThemeProvider`, `useTheme()` (the active theme + appearance prefs) and `makeStyles((theme) => ({...}))`. Every component declares `const useStyles = makeStyles(...)` at the bottom and calls `const styles = useStyles()` inside; never use a module-level `StyleSheet.create` with colors, or the component won't follow light/dark and the accent.
- `src/lib/appearance.ts` (+ `appearanceStorage.ts`) — look & layout prefs saved on the device: theme mode, color theme (`palette`), accent, how Today is grouped, the quick-win limit. `src/lib/taskSections.ts` groups Today's tasks for the chosen layout; `src/lib/taskRhythm.ts` learns how often repeat tasks get done and feeds the add sheet's one-tap "usuals"; `src/lib/dueOptions.ts` turns the add sheet's `DueChoice` (one exact day, or a flexible window: any day / this week / next week) into `due_date`/`due_kind` (`resolveDueChoice`): an exact day is `"on"`, a week is a `"by"` deadline on its Sunday. `src/lib/dayPicker.ts` is the pure calendar math (seven-day strip, Monday-first month grid) behind `DayPicker`, the presentational "When?" control.
- `src/components/AddTaskSheet.tsx` — the only place a single task is typed in: opens on the usuals (one tap adds, with Undo), and typing something new asks what / how long / when, with category, energy, time of day and a fixed start behind one link. Fields the user set by hand are never overwritten by task memory.
- `supabase/functions/_shared/domain/brainDump/` — Brain Dump contract and rules, pure: `aiResult.ts` (zod schema the AI must satisfy; dates are *described* by the model, never computed), `resolveDate.ts`, `normalize.ts` (AI proposal → `TaskDraft`s using history, work hours, categories), `reviewPolicy.ts`, `types.ts` (API response). Category ids come from `@domain/categories`; `src/lib/categories.ts` only adds visuals.
- `supabase/functions/_shared/server/` — Deno-only (never imported by the app): `ai/` (vendor-neutral `LLMClient` + Anthropic/OpenAI adapters, `AIProvider` feature interface, `providerFactory.ts` — the one place that turns secrets into an `AIProvider`: `AI_PROVIDER=rules` gives `rulesAIProvider.ts`, the free rule-based parser, anything else a configured vendor — versioned prompts — bump the prompt version on any prompt change), `brainDump/` (request schema, repository interface + Supabase impl, service, HTTP handler), `http/apiError.ts`. Tests use `node:assert/strict` + `Deno.test` (`deno task test` in `supabase/functions`).
- Rule-based Brain Dump: `@domain/brainDump/rulesParser` (`parseBrainDumpWithRules`) is pure and returns the same `BrainDumpAiResult` a model must, so normalize/review/commit don't know the difference. It powers free mode and the web demo. Its word patterns use `words()` (Unicode-aware boundaries; plain `\b` breaks on "venerdì"/"yarın"). After changing a rule, bump `RULES_PARSER_VERSION`, add a case to `rulesParser.test.ts` and run `deno task eval --provider rules`.
- Demo code (`src/lib/demo/`) is loaded with `require` only when `isDemoMode` (see `src/lib/supabase.ts`), so a real build never runs it. Don't import anything from `src/lib/demo/` statically outside that folder.
- AI rule: AI output is a proposal. It is schema-validated and normalized, and tasks are only ever created via `create_task` (directly or through `commit_brain_dump`). AI never writes to the database, never picks ids or computes dates. API keys only live in Supabase secrets.
- `supabase/functions/_eval/` — evaluation set and runner for choosing/regressing AI models (`deno task eval`); not deployed.
- `supabase/migrations/` — source of truth for the DB schema, RLS policies and `public.create_task`. Never edit an applied migration; add a new timestamped file and mirror column/enum changes in `@domain/task`. DB tests live in `supabase/tests/database/` (pgTAP).
- Tests: `npm test` (Jest, pinned to Europe/Rome via `jest.globalSetup.js`), `npm run typecheck`, `npm run db:test`.

## Conventions

- Naming: `camelCase` for functions/variables, `PascalCase` for components/types, snake_case only for DB columns (matches Postgres/Supabase convention) — mirrored exactly in `@domain/task` (`Task`, `TaskDraft`) and re-exported from `src/lib/types.ts`.
- Every exported function/component gets a 1–2 sentence comment explaining *why*, not a restatement of the code.
- Timing values are always one of `"before_work" | "after_work" | "anytime"` (see `TaskTiming` in `@domain/task`) — don't introduce new string literals for this (or for priority, energy, due kind, source) without updating the type and the DB check constraint in a new migration.
- Dates: a task's `due_date` is a local calendar day (`"YYYY-MM-DD"`). Compute "today" with `toLocalDateKey(new Date())` from `@domain/dates` — never `toISOString().slice(0, 10)`, which is UTC.
- What shows on Today is decided only by `isPendingToday` (`@domain/todayTasks`): undated tasks, tasks due today or overdue, and every task with a deadline (`due_kind: "by"`), since it can be done any day before it. A task set for one exact later day (`"on"`) is hidden until that day.
- Task status is `"pending" | "someday" | "done"`. `"someday"` tasks are parked: every daily list goes through `isPendingToday`, which only accepts `"pending"`, so they stay hidden without extra filtering.
- `postponed_count` is owned by the `tasks_track_postponement` database trigger (it adds one when an unfinished task's `due_date` moves later, and clears it when a task comes back from `"someday"`). Never write it from the app; to postpone a task, just update its `due_date`.
- Fallbacks for settings that are still loading come from `DEFAULT_SETTINGS` in `src/lib/settingsApi.ts`, never an inline number.
- Category values are a category `id` from `src/lib/categories.ts` or `null` — always look categories up via `getCategory(id)` (handles `null` and unknown/deleted ids) rather than indexing `CATEGORIES` directly.
- A task's `scheduled_time` (`"HH:MM"` or `null`) is what enables Focus mode instead of the range timer — it's independent of `timing`; a task can have both a timing bucket and a fixed time.
- Keep screens dumb: fetching/mutation logic belongs in `src/hooks/*` or `src/lib/*Api.ts`, not inside a screen component.
- Icons: use `Ionicons` from `@expo/vector-icons`. The current design mostly avoids icons in favor of plain text (pills, tab bar) — only add an icon where the design shows one.
- Color: each color theme uses one accent-based palette everywhere (no per-timing color coding on task rows); the fixed brand colors in `PALETTE` are only for expressive surfaces such as the Today hero — category tags are the one place distinct accent hues (`theme.colors.indigo/purple/warning/pink`) are used, to tell categories apart at a glance.
